1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,787
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,967 --> 00:00:03,070
You got the raise.

3
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:04,609
Congratulations.

4
00:00:04,789 --> 00:00:07,193
Five thousand dollars more a year,

5
00:00:07,193 --> 00:00:07,900
maybe ten.

6
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,193
And then something strange happens.

7
00:00:10,373 --> 00:00:11,169
A year later,

8
00:00:11,169 --> 00:00:13,311
your savings look exactly the same.

9
00:00:13,491 --> 00:00:15,638
Your stress looks exactly the same.

10
00:00:15,818 --> 00:00:17,304
The raise just vanished.

11
00:00:17,484 --> 00:00:20,150
Today, twenty ways your raise disappears,

12
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:22,036
and how to keep the next one.

13
00:00:22,385 --> 00:00:26,415
Lifestyle inflation, sometimes called lifestyle creep,

14
00:00:26,415 --> 00:00:29,250
is when your spending rises every time

15
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:30,518
your income does,

16
00:00:30,518 --> 00:00:33,130
so a higher salary never turns into

17
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:34,100
more savings.

18
00:00:34,449 --> 00:00:37,787
Lifestyle inflation is sneaky because none of

19
00:00:37,787 --> 00:00:39,790
it feels like overspending.

20
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:41,780
It feels like finally living at the

21
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:42,815
level you've earned.

22
00:00:42,995 --> 00:00:44,771
A slightly nicer apartment.

23
00:00:44,951 --> 00:00:45,961
A newer car.

24
00:00:46,141 --> 00:00:47,314
Better groceries.

25
00:00:47,494 --> 00:00:48,666
More dinners out.

26
00:00:48,846 --> 00:00:51,238
Each upgrade is small and reasonable.

27
00:00:51,418 --> 00:00:54,378
Together, they can swallow an entire raise,

28
00:00:54,378 --> 00:00:55,342
and then some.

29
00:00:55,691 --> 00:00:57,874
We'll look at it from three angles.

30
00:00:58,054 --> 00:00:59,595
Angle one, the numbers:

31
00:00:59,595 --> 00:01:01,069
the first seven leaks,

32
00:01:01,069 --> 00:01:03,547
where the money from a raise actually

33
00:01:03,547 --> 00:01:03,882
goes.

34
00:01:04,062 --> 00:01:06,131
Angle two, the psychology:

35
00:01:06,131 --> 00:01:10,030
why upgrades feel necessary and become permanent.

36
00:01:10,210 --> 00:01:11,217
And angle three,

37
00:01:11,217 --> 00:01:13,610
the playbook: the leaks that hurt your

38
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:14,681
long term wealth,

39
00:01:14,681 --> 00:01:16,633
and a simple rule that lets you

40
00:01:16,633 --> 00:01:18,774
enjoy a raise and still get richer

41
00:01:18,774 --> 00:01:19,277
from it.

42
00:01:19,627 --> 00:01:21,811
And if you've ever wondered why people

43
00:01:21,811 --> 00:01:23,765
earning twice as much as you still

44
00:01:23,765 --> 00:01:24,799
say they're broke,

45
00:01:24,799 --> 00:01:26,408
this video answers that too.

46
00:01:26,588 --> 00:01:28,318
Let's start with the numbers.

47
00:01:28,667 --> 00:01:30,680
Every number here comes from a named

48
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:31,071
source.

49
00:01:31,251 --> 00:01:33,996
Spending patterns come from the Bureau of

50
00:01:33,996 --> 00:01:37,010
Labor Statistics Consumer Expenditure Survey,

51
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:40,023
which tracks how American households spend at

52
00:01:40,023 --> 00:01:41,630
different income levels.

53
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:43,819
Tax rules come from the IRS.

54
00:01:43,999 --> 00:01:46,655
Retirement plan rules come from the IRS

55
00:01:46,655 --> 00:01:48,561
and the Department of Labor.

56
00:01:48,741 --> 00:01:50,156
Examples use stated,

57
00:01:50,156 --> 00:01:53,057
simple math, like a seven percent average

58
00:01:53,057 --> 00:01:54,047
yearly return.

59
00:01:54,227 --> 00:01:55,707
That's an illustration,

60
00:01:55,707 --> 00:01:56,607
not a promise.

61
00:01:56,787 --> 00:01:58,170
This is education,

62
00:01:58,170 --> 00:01:59,782
not financial advice.

63
00:02:00,132 --> 00:02:03,470
The Bureau of Labor Statistics Consumer Expenditure

64
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:05,369
Survey shows a clear pattern.

65
00:02:05,549 --> 00:02:08,453
Households with higher incomes spend more in

66
00:02:08,453 --> 00:02:09,906
almost every category,

67
00:02:09,906 --> 00:02:12,217
from housing to food away from home

68
00:02:12,217 --> 00:02:13,339
to entertainment.

69
00:02:13,688 --> 00:02:16,023
The phrase keeping up with the Joneses

70
00:02:16,023 --> 00:02:18,296
comes from a comic strip that started

71
00:02:18,296 --> 00:02:19,586
in nineteen thirteen.

72
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:22,181
It followed a family desperately trying to

73
00:02:22,181 --> 00:02:24,423
match the lifestyle of their neighbors,

74
00:02:24,423 --> 00:02:26,895
the Joneses, who were never actually shown.

75
00:02:27,075 --> 00:02:28,039
That's the joke.

76
00:02:28,219 --> 00:02:30,396
The people we compete with are often

77
00:02:30,396 --> 00:02:31,727
people we barely know,

78
00:02:31,727 --> 00:02:33,965
and whose real finances we never see.

79
00:02:34,315 --> 00:02:35,367
A century later,

80
00:02:35,367 --> 00:02:37,601
the Joneses aren't just next door.

81
00:02:37,781 --> 00:02:39,070
They're on every screen.

82
00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:42,974
Friends, influencers, celebrities and strangers,

83
00:02:42,974 --> 00:02:45,380
all showing their best moments.

84
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:48,556
Economists have found that people's satisfaction with

85
00:02:48,556 --> 00:02:50,590
their income often depends on how it

86
00:02:50,590 --> 00:02:52,456
compares with others around them,

87
00:02:52,456 --> 00:02:54,151
not just on the amount itself.

88
00:02:54,331 --> 00:02:56,091
That's why a raise can feel great

89
00:02:56,091 --> 00:02:58,012
until you see what someone else got.

90
00:02:58,361 --> 00:02:59,948
The good news is that you can

91
00:02:59,948 --> 00:03:01,206
choose your comparison.

92
00:03:01,386 --> 00:03:03,785
Instead of comparing your lifestyle with other

93
00:03:03,785 --> 00:03:06,132
people's, compare your savings rate this year

94
00:03:06,132 --> 00:03:07,853
with your savings rate last year.

95
00:03:08,033 --> 00:03:09,874
That's a race you can actually win,

96
00:03:09,874 --> 00:03:11,662
and it's the only one that changes

97
00:03:11,662 --> 00:03:12,294
your future.

98
00:03:12,643 --> 00:03:13,340
Angle one.

99
00:03:13,520 --> 00:03:14,263
The numbers.

100
00:03:14,443 --> 00:03:16,427
Where does the money from a raise

101
00:03:16,427 --> 00:03:17,148
actually go?

102
00:03:17,328 --> 00:03:19,987
Let's follow a five thousand dollar raise.

103
00:03:20,336 --> 00:03:21,521
Leak number one.

104
00:03:21,701 --> 00:03:23,593
Taxes take the first bite.

105
00:03:23,943 --> 00:03:26,099
A five thousand dollar raise is not

106
00:03:26,099 --> 00:03:28,378
five thousand dollars in your pocket.

107
00:03:28,558 --> 00:03:30,040
Federal income tax,

108
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:32,301
Social Security and Medicare,

109
00:03:32,301 --> 00:03:35,030
and in many places state income tax

110
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:36,511
all come out first.

111
00:03:36,691 --> 00:03:38,448
Depending on your situation,

112
00:03:38,448 --> 00:03:40,205
the take home part of a five

113
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:42,715
thousand dollar raise might be closer to

114
00:03:42,715 --> 00:03:45,538
thirty five hundred or four thousand dollars.

115
00:03:45,718 --> 00:03:47,854
That's around three hundred dollars a month.

116
00:03:48,204 --> 00:03:50,809
This matters because people mentally spend the

117
00:03:50,809 --> 00:03:51,432
full raise.

118
00:03:51,612 --> 00:03:53,864
They think, I make five thousand more,

119
00:03:53,864 --> 00:03:55,641
so I can afford a four hundred

120
00:03:55,641 --> 00:03:56,767
dollar car payment.

121
00:03:56,947 --> 00:03:58,856
But the real extra money might be

122
00:03:58,856 --> 00:04:00,128
three hundred a month.

123
00:04:00,308 --> 00:04:02,521
The upgrade is already bigger than the

124
00:04:02,521 --> 00:04:03,861
raise before it starts.

125
00:04:04,210 --> 00:04:05,140
And by the way,

126
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:06,938
a common myth is that a raise

127
00:04:06,938 --> 00:04:09,046
can push you into a higher bracket

128
00:04:09,046 --> 00:04:10,782
and make you take home less.

129
00:04:10,962 --> 00:04:12,250
That's not how it works.

130
00:04:12,430 --> 00:04:13,644
In the United States,

131
00:04:13,644 --> 00:04:16,072
only the dollars above a bracket threshold

132
00:04:16,072 --> 00:04:17,748
are taxed at the higher rate.

133
00:04:17,928 --> 00:04:20,388
A raise almost always means more take

134
00:04:20,388 --> 00:04:22,849
home pay, just less than the headline

135
00:04:22,849 --> 00:04:23,315
number.

136
00:04:23,495 --> 00:04:25,462
Try this: after your next raise,

137
00:04:25,462 --> 00:04:28,166
look at your actual paycheck before planning

138
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:28,719
anything.

139
00:04:29,069 --> 00:04:31,123
A five thousand dollar raise,

140
00:04:31,123 --> 00:04:32,893
after federal income tax,

141
00:04:32,893 --> 00:04:35,868
Social Security, Medicare and state taxes,

142
00:04:35,868 --> 00:04:38,842
might add only about three hundred dollars

143
00:04:38,842 --> 00:04:40,612
a month to take home pay,

144
00:04:40,612 --> 00:04:42,595
depending on your situation.

145
00:04:42,945 --> 00:04:44,025
Leak number two.

146
00:04:44,205 --> 00:04:45,540
The housing upgrade.

147
00:04:45,890 --> 00:04:48,036
Housing is the biggest line in most

148
00:04:48,036 --> 00:04:50,243
budgets, and the most common place a

149
00:04:50,243 --> 00:04:50,918
raise goes.

150
00:04:51,098 --> 00:04:52,564
A nicer neighborhood,

151
00:04:52,564 --> 00:04:53,752
one more bedroom,

152
00:04:53,752 --> 00:04:55,289
a building with a gym.

153
00:04:55,469 --> 00:04:57,461
Moving up two hundred or three hundred

154
00:04:57,461 --> 00:04:59,139
dollars a month in rent can feel

155
00:04:59,139 --> 00:05:00,449
like a reasonable reward.

156
00:05:00,629 --> 00:05:01,858
And just like that,

157
00:05:01,858 --> 00:05:03,603
most of the raise is spent,

158
00:05:03,603 --> 00:05:05,607
every month, for as long as you

159
00:05:05,607 --> 00:05:06,318
live there.

160
00:05:06,668 --> 00:05:08,062
And it's never just rent.

161
00:05:08,242 --> 00:05:10,861
A bigger place means more furniture,

162
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:14,063
higher utilities, higher renter's insurance,

163
00:05:14,063 --> 00:05:15,591
maybe a parking spot.

164
00:05:15,771 --> 00:05:18,381
A house means higher property taxes and

165
00:05:18,381 --> 00:05:19,184
maintenance.

166
00:05:19,364 --> 00:05:21,721
The housing upgrade is usually the leak

167
00:05:21,721 --> 00:05:24,198
that pulls several other leaks along with

168
00:05:24,198 --> 00:05:24,380
it.

169
00:05:24,729 --> 00:05:26,849
Try this: when your lease comes up

170
00:05:26,849 --> 00:05:27,722
after a raise,

171
00:05:27,722 --> 00:05:29,281
ask a different question.

172
00:05:29,461 --> 00:05:31,492
Not can I afford a nicer place?

173
00:05:31,672 --> 00:05:33,483
But would a nicer place make me

174
00:05:33,483 --> 00:05:35,994
happier than having an extra three thousand

175
00:05:35,994 --> 00:05:37,512
dollars a year in savings?

176
00:05:37,692 --> 00:05:39,898
Sometimes the answer is yes.

177
00:05:40,078 --> 00:05:41,378
Often, it isn't.

178
00:05:41,728 --> 00:05:42,913
Leak number three.

179
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:44,230
The car upgrade.

180
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:46,691
A raise often comes with a new

181
00:05:46,691 --> 00:05:46,972
car.

182
00:05:47,152 --> 00:05:48,150
The old one works,

183
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:49,426
but it's getting older,

184
00:05:49,426 --> 00:05:51,367
and now you can afford the payment.

185
00:05:51,547 --> 00:05:53,701
Remember from my car payment video,

186
00:05:53,701 --> 00:05:55,917
the average new car payment has been

187
00:05:55,917 --> 00:05:58,503
around seven hundred fifty dollars a month

188
00:05:58,503 --> 00:05:59,488
in recent years.

189
00:05:59,668 --> 00:06:01,753
Even a modest upgrade can eat two

190
00:06:01,753 --> 00:06:04,281
or three hundred dollars of your monthly

191
00:06:04,281 --> 00:06:04,660
raise.

192
00:06:05,010 --> 00:06:07,184
Add higher insurance for a newer,

193
00:06:07,184 --> 00:06:08,435
more expensive car,

194
00:06:08,435 --> 00:06:10,806
and the upgrade can easily cost more

195
00:06:10,806 --> 00:06:12,255
than the raise itself.

196
00:06:12,435 --> 00:06:14,492
The raise lasts as long as your

197
00:06:14,492 --> 00:06:14,757
job.

198
00:06:14,937 --> 00:06:17,187
The car payment lasts five to seven

199
00:06:17,187 --> 00:06:19,244
years, even if your job changes.

200
00:06:19,594 --> 00:06:22,081
Try this: keep your current car until

201
00:06:22,081 --> 00:06:23,762
it truly needs replacing,

202
00:06:23,762 --> 00:06:26,383
and start paying yourself a car payment

203
00:06:26,383 --> 00:06:28,197
now into a savings account.

204
00:06:28,377 --> 00:06:29,655
When the time comes,

205
00:06:29,655 --> 00:06:31,570
you'll buy the next car with a

206
00:06:31,570 --> 00:06:32,656
big down payment,

207
00:06:32,656 --> 00:06:34,763
or cash, and your raise will have

208
00:06:34,763 --> 00:06:36,806
built savings instead of a loan.

209
00:06:37,156 --> 00:06:38,259
Leak number four.

210
00:06:38,439 --> 00:06:39,658
Subscriptions.

211
00:06:40,008 --> 00:06:42,313
Streaming services, music,

212
00:06:42,313 --> 00:06:44,086
cloud storage, apps,

213
00:06:44,086 --> 00:06:46,479
meal kits, fitness classes,

214
00:06:46,479 --> 00:06:48,252
premium memberships.

215
00:06:48,432 --> 00:06:49,666
Each one is ten,

216
00:06:49,666 --> 00:06:52,367
fifteen, or twenty dollars a month.

217
00:06:52,547 --> 00:06:53,415
With a raise,

218
00:06:53,415 --> 00:06:55,752
adding one more feels like nothing.

219
00:06:55,932 --> 00:06:58,308
But eight or ten subscriptions can easily

220
00:06:58,308 --> 00:07:00,511
reach one hundred fifty to two hundred

221
00:07:00,511 --> 00:07:01,438
dollars a month,

222
00:07:01,438 --> 00:07:03,525
which is over two thousand dollars a

223
00:07:03,525 --> 00:07:03,815
year.

224
00:07:04,164 --> 00:07:06,615
Subscriptions are designed to be easy to

225
00:07:06,615 --> 00:07:08,147
start and easy to forget.

226
00:07:08,327 --> 00:07:10,869
Free trials that convert automatically.

227
00:07:11,049 --> 00:07:13,288
Annual renewals that arrive when you're not

228
00:07:13,288 --> 00:07:14,172
paying attention.

229
00:07:14,352 --> 00:07:16,616
Price increases that happen quietly.

230
00:07:16,796 --> 00:07:19,355
Many people pay for services they haven't

231
00:07:19,355 --> 00:07:20,291
used in months.

232
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:23,430
Try this: list every subscription from your

233
00:07:23,430 --> 00:07:25,052
bank and card statements.

234
00:07:25,232 --> 00:07:27,288
Cancel anything you haven't used in the

235
00:07:27,288 --> 00:07:27,868
last month.

236
00:07:28,048 --> 00:07:31,182
Rotate streaming services instead of keeping them

237
00:07:31,182 --> 00:07:31,438
all.

238
00:07:31,618 --> 00:07:33,651
One month for one service,

239
00:07:33,651 --> 00:07:34,590
then switch.

240
00:07:34,770 --> 00:07:36,709
You'll still have plenty to watch.

241
00:07:37,059 --> 00:07:38,243
Leak number five.

242
00:07:38,423 --> 00:07:40,106
Eating out and delivery.

243
00:07:40,457 --> 00:07:41,784
When money is tight,

244
00:07:41,784 --> 00:07:42,581
people cook.

245
00:07:42,761 --> 00:07:43,940
When money loosens,

246
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:46,360
takeout and restaurants become a habit.

247
00:07:46,540 --> 00:07:48,846
The BLS data shows food away from

248
00:07:48,846 --> 00:07:50,522
home rising with income.

249
00:07:50,702 --> 00:07:52,216
And delivery adds fees,

250
00:07:52,216 --> 00:07:53,664
tips and menu markups,

251
00:07:53,664 --> 00:07:55,639
so the same meal can cost much

252
00:07:55,639 --> 00:07:57,942
more than eating at the restaurant,

253
00:07:57,942 --> 00:07:59,654
and far more than cooking.

254
00:08:00,003 --> 00:08:01,304
Eating out isn't bad.

255
00:08:01,484 --> 00:08:03,060
It's one of the best ways to

256
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:04,862
spend time with people you love.

257
00:08:05,042 --> 00:08:07,261
The problem is when it replaces home

258
00:08:07,261 --> 00:08:08,556
cooking out of habit,

259
00:08:08,556 --> 00:08:09,234
not choice.

260
00:08:09,414 --> 00:08:11,572
A few extra delivery orders a week

261
00:08:11,572 --> 00:08:13,921
can add up to several hundred dollars

262
00:08:13,921 --> 00:08:14,429
a month.

263
00:08:14,779 --> 00:08:16,994
Try this: decide how many meals out

264
00:08:16,994 --> 00:08:18,513
per week fit your goals,

265
00:08:18,513 --> 00:08:19,906
then enjoy them fully,

266
00:08:19,906 --> 00:08:20,792
without guilt.

267
00:08:20,972 --> 00:08:22,264
For everything else,

268
00:08:22,264 --> 00:08:23,297
keep a few easy,

269
00:08:23,297 --> 00:08:25,621
fast meals at home for tired nights,

270
00:08:25,621 --> 00:08:28,333
which is when most delivery orders happen.

271
00:08:28,683 --> 00:08:29,926
Leak number six.

272
00:08:30,106 --> 00:08:32,648
Premium versions of everyday things.

273
00:08:32,999 --> 00:08:34,589
This is the quietest leak.

274
00:08:34,769 --> 00:08:35,761
After a raise,

275
00:08:35,761 --> 00:08:37,532
the grocery cart changes.

276
00:08:37,712 --> 00:08:40,092
Organic versions, name brands,

277
00:08:40,092 --> 00:08:42,472
fancier coffee, nicer shampoo,

278
00:08:42,472 --> 00:08:43,424
better wine.

279
00:08:43,604 --> 00:08:45,392
None of these choices is big.

280
00:08:45,572 --> 00:08:47,817
But a ten to twenty percent increase

281
00:08:47,817 --> 00:08:50,311
on groceries and household items can add

282
00:08:50,311 --> 00:08:52,306
up to over one hundred dollars a

283
00:08:52,306 --> 00:08:53,490
month for a family.

284
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,901
Some upgrades are worth it to you,

285
00:08:55,901 --> 00:08:56,871
and that's fine.

286
00:08:57,051 --> 00:08:59,539
The leak happens when you upgrade everything

287
00:08:59,539 --> 00:09:02,763
automatically, without asking which ones actually matter.

288
00:09:02,943 --> 00:09:05,384
Many store brands are made to similar

289
00:09:05,384 --> 00:09:07,034
standards as name brands,

290
00:09:07,034 --> 00:09:08,156
at a lower price.

291
00:09:08,505 --> 00:09:11,698
Try this: pick three things where premium

292
00:09:11,698 --> 00:09:13,566
truly makes you happier,

293
00:09:13,566 --> 00:09:15,902
maybe coffee, a good mattress,

294
00:09:15,902 --> 00:09:16,992
quality shoes.

295
00:09:17,172 --> 00:09:18,473
Spend well on those.

296
00:09:18,653 --> 00:09:19,741
For everything else,

297
00:09:19,741 --> 00:09:21,265
buy the good enough version.

298
00:09:21,445 --> 00:09:23,139
That's how you upgrade your life without

299
00:09:23,139 --> 00:09:24,324
upgrading your whole budget.

300
00:09:24,674 --> 00:09:25,859
Leak number seven.

301
00:09:26,039 --> 00:09:27,443
Constant tech upgrades.

302
00:09:27,793 --> 00:09:29,330
New phones, laptops,

303
00:09:29,330 --> 00:09:33,017
watches, headphones and gaming gear arrive every

304
00:09:33,017 --> 00:09:36,320
year, and carriers make upgrading easy with

305
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:38,010
monthly payment plans.

306
00:09:38,190 --> 00:09:40,890
A thousand dollar phone spread over two

307
00:09:40,890 --> 00:09:43,175
or three years looks like a small

308
00:09:43,175 --> 00:09:45,875
monthly charge, and the next upgrade is

309
00:09:45,875 --> 00:09:47,536
always being advertised.

310
00:09:47,886 --> 00:09:50,625
Modern devices often last many years with

311
00:09:50,625 --> 00:09:52,496
a new battery and good care.

312
00:09:52,676 --> 00:09:55,234
Upgrading every year or two instead of

313
00:09:55,234 --> 00:09:57,657
every four or five can cost hundreds

314
00:09:57,657 --> 00:09:58,869
of dollars a year,

315
00:09:58,869 --> 00:10:01,697
often for features you barely notice after

316
00:10:01,697 --> 00:10:02,707
the first week.

317
00:10:03,057 --> 00:10:05,656
Try this: keep your devices until they

318
00:10:05,656 --> 00:10:07,366
stop doing what you need,

319
00:10:07,366 --> 00:10:09,419
not until a new one comes out.

320
00:10:09,599 --> 00:10:10,933
When you do upgrade,

321
00:10:10,933 --> 00:10:13,267
consider the previous year's model,

322
00:10:13,267 --> 00:10:15,668
which often costs much less and does

323
00:10:15,668 --> 00:10:18,202
almost everything the newest one does.

324
00:10:18,552 --> 00:10:20,532
Let's add up the first seven leaks

325
00:10:20,532 --> 00:10:21,988
with very modest numbers.

326
00:10:22,168 --> 00:10:23,747
Higher rent, two hundred.

327
00:10:23,927 --> 00:10:25,518
Car upgrade, two hundred.

328
00:10:25,698 --> 00:10:27,671
Subscriptions, fifty.

329
00:10:27,851 --> 00:10:28,972
More eating out,

330
00:10:28,972 --> 00:10:29,813
one hundred.

331
00:10:29,993 --> 00:10:32,037
Premium groceries, fifty.

332
00:10:32,217 --> 00:10:33,308
Tech, thirty.

333
00:10:33,488 --> 00:10:35,636
That's six hundred thirty dollars a month.

334
00:10:35,816 --> 00:10:38,235
Remember, the raise only added about three

335
00:10:38,235 --> 00:10:40,135
hundred dollars of take home pay.

336
00:10:40,315 --> 00:10:42,691
The lifestyle grew twice as fast as

337
00:10:42,691 --> 00:10:43,438
the income.

338
00:10:43,788 --> 00:10:45,949
A five thousand dollar raise can add

339
00:10:45,949 --> 00:10:48,230
about three hundred dollars a month to

340
00:10:48,230 --> 00:10:49,070
take home pay.

341
00:10:49,250 --> 00:10:51,532
Seven small upgrades can add over six

342
00:10:51,532 --> 00:10:53,999
hundred dollars a month in new spending.

343
00:10:54,349 --> 00:10:56,143
That's how people earn more and end

344
00:10:56,143 --> 00:10:56,810
up with less.

345
00:10:56,990 --> 00:10:59,556
Now, why do these upgrades feel so

346
00:10:59,556 --> 00:11:00,310
necessary?

347
00:11:00,490 --> 00:11:01,431
Angle two.

348
00:11:01,781 --> 00:11:02,651
Angle two.

349
00:11:02,831 --> 00:11:04,016
The psychology.

350
00:11:04,196 --> 00:11:06,719
Eight reasons upgrades feel necessary,

351
00:11:06,719 --> 00:11:08,445
and then become permanent.

352
00:11:08,795 --> 00:11:09,770
Leak number eight.

353
00:11:09,950 --> 00:11:11,239
The hedonic treadmill.

354
00:11:11,589 --> 00:11:14,740
Psychologists use the term hedonic treadmill to

355
00:11:14,740 --> 00:11:17,355
describe how people adapt to changes in

356
00:11:17,355 --> 00:11:18,160
their lives.

357
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:19,540
A new apartment,

358
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:21,339
a new car or a pay raise

359
00:11:21,339 --> 00:11:22,914
feels great at first.

360
00:11:23,094 --> 00:11:24,151
But within months,

361
00:11:24,151 --> 00:11:25,207
it becomes normal.

362
00:11:25,387 --> 00:11:26,919
The happiness boost fades,

363
00:11:26,919 --> 00:11:28,863
and we start looking for the next

364
00:11:28,863 --> 00:11:29,335
upgrade.

365
00:11:29,685 --> 00:11:32,925
This is why lifestyle inflation never feels

366
00:11:32,925 --> 00:11:33,829
like enough.

367
00:11:34,009 --> 00:11:35,040
You run faster,

368
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:36,552
earn more, spend more,

369
00:11:36,552 --> 00:11:39,232
and your happiness stays roughly in the

370
00:11:39,232 --> 00:11:39,988
same place.

371
00:11:40,168 --> 00:11:42,966
Meanwhile, the costs stay locked in.

372
00:11:43,146 --> 00:11:45,056
You're running on a treadmill that keeps

373
00:11:45,056 --> 00:11:46,153
getting more expensive.

374
00:11:46,504 --> 00:11:49,193
Try this: before any permanent upgrade,

375
00:11:49,193 --> 00:11:51,331
ask, how will I feel about this

376
00:11:51,331 --> 00:11:52,297
in six months,

377
00:11:52,297 --> 00:11:53,470
when it's normal?

378
00:11:53,650 --> 00:11:55,272
If the honest answer is that you

379
00:11:55,272 --> 00:11:56,337
won't notice it much,

380
00:11:56,337 --> 00:11:57,098
keep the money.

381
00:11:57,278 --> 00:12:00,268
Spend instead on things that keep giving,

382
00:12:00,268 --> 00:12:02,091
like experiences, health,

383
00:12:02,091 --> 00:12:04,279
and time with people you love.

384
00:12:04,629 --> 00:12:05,778
Leak number nine.

385
00:12:05,958 --> 00:12:07,131
A new peer group.

386
00:12:07,481 --> 00:12:09,923
Raises often come with a new role,

387
00:12:09,923 --> 00:12:12,078
a new company or a new circle.

388
00:12:12,258 --> 00:12:14,290
And new peers bring new norms.

389
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:16,630
Different restaurants for lunch,

390
00:12:16,630 --> 00:12:18,182
more expensive hobbies,

391
00:12:18,182 --> 00:12:20,275
nicer vacations, bigger houses.

392
00:12:20,455 --> 00:12:22,251
What was a luxury in your old

393
00:12:22,251 --> 00:12:24,356
circle is ordinary in the new one.

394
00:12:24,706 --> 00:12:26,621
It's natural to want to fit in.

395
00:12:26,801 --> 00:12:28,829
But your new peers might have different

396
00:12:28,829 --> 00:12:29,297
finances.

397
00:12:29,477 --> 00:12:30,371
Some earn more.

398
00:12:30,551 --> 00:12:32,142
Some have family money.

399
00:12:32,322 --> 00:12:33,680
Some carry a lot of debt.

400
00:12:33,860 --> 00:12:36,470
Matching their lifestyle with your income is

401
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:38,308
one of the fastest ways to lose

402
00:12:38,308 --> 00:12:38,783
a raise.

403
00:12:39,133 --> 00:12:41,652
Try this: join in on your terms.

404
00:12:41,832 --> 00:12:43,922
Say yes to the events that matter,

405
00:12:43,922 --> 00:12:46,012
suggest cheaper options sometimes,

406
00:12:46,012 --> 00:12:48,287
and don't apologize for having goals.

407
00:12:48,467 --> 00:12:50,976
You'll find that many people respect someone

408
00:12:50,976 --> 00:12:52,972
who's clear about their priorities.

409
00:12:53,322 --> 00:12:54,610
Leak number ten.

410
00:12:54,790 --> 00:12:57,089
Treat yourself becomes every week.

411
00:12:57,439 --> 00:12:58,797
Treats are great.

412
00:12:58,977 --> 00:13:00,695
The problem is frequency.

413
00:13:00,875 --> 00:13:02,252
When a treat is rare,

414
00:13:02,252 --> 00:13:03,104
it's special.

415
00:13:03,284 --> 00:13:04,817
When it becomes weekly,

416
00:13:04,817 --> 00:13:06,082
it's just spending.

417
00:13:06,262 --> 00:13:07,327
After a raise,

418
00:13:07,327 --> 00:13:09,153
treats tend to multiply.

419
00:13:09,333 --> 00:13:10,452
The Friday dinner,

420
00:13:10,452 --> 00:13:12,069
the weekend shopping trip,

421
00:13:12,069 --> 00:13:14,431
the random online order because it was

422
00:13:14,431 --> 00:13:15,115
a long day.

423
00:13:15,465 --> 00:13:17,479
And the more often you treat yourself,

424
00:13:17,479 --> 00:13:19,175
the less each treat feels like a

425
00:13:19,175 --> 00:13:19,493
treat.

426
00:13:19,673 --> 00:13:21,484
That's the hedonic treadmill again.

427
00:13:21,664 --> 00:13:23,162
You need more to feel the same.

428
00:13:23,342 --> 00:13:25,645
Rare treats are both cheaper and more

429
00:13:25,645 --> 00:13:26,268
enjoyable.

430
00:13:26,618 --> 00:13:29,105
Try this: give yourself a set amount

431
00:13:29,105 --> 00:13:30,763
of fun money each month,

432
00:13:30,763 --> 00:13:32,835
and spend it any way you like,

433
00:13:32,835 --> 00:13:33,595
guilt free.

434
00:13:33,775 --> 00:13:34,812
When it's gone,

435
00:13:34,812 --> 00:13:36,678
it's gone until next month.

436
00:13:36,858 --> 00:13:39,319
Limits make treats feel special again.

437
00:13:39,669 --> 00:13:40,748
Leak number eleven.

438
00:13:40,928 --> 00:13:42,368
Paying for convenience.

439
00:13:42,718 --> 00:13:43,780
With more income,

440
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:45,655
it's tempting to pay for time.

441
00:13:45,835 --> 00:13:48,240
Cleaning services, delivery,

442
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:50,386
rideshares, dry cleaning,

443
00:13:50,386 --> 00:13:51,675
grocery pickup.

444
00:13:51,855 --> 00:13:54,167
Some of these can genuinely be worth

445
00:13:54,167 --> 00:13:56,480
it, especially if they free time for

446
00:13:56,480 --> 00:13:58,728
family, rest or higher paying work.

447
00:13:58,908 --> 00:14:01,734
Research on time and happiness suggests that

448
00:14:01,734 --> 00:14:03,981
buying time can improve well being.

449
00:14:04,332 --> 00:14:07,469
The leak happens when convenience spending isn't

450
00:14:07,469 --> 00:14:09,103
a choice, it's a default.

451
00:14:09,283 --> 00:14:11,739
Rideshares when a short walk or bus

452
00:14:11,739 --> 00:14:12,511
works fine.

453
00:14:12,691 --> 00:14:14,887
Delivery for things you'll pass on the

454
00:14:14,887 --> 00:14:15,408
way home.

455
00:14:15,588 --> 00:14:18,417
Convenience is easy to justify one purchase

456
00:14:18,417 --> 00:14:19,074
at a time,

457
00:14:19,074 --> 00:14:20,719
and hard to see in total.

458
00:14:21,070 --> 00:14:23,782
Try this: choose one or two convenience

459
00:14:23,782 --> 00:14:27,190
services that genuinely give you meaningful time,

460
00:14:27,190 --> 00:14:28,163
and keep them.

461
00:14:28,343 --> 00:14:29,423
Drop the rest.

462
00:14:29,603 --> 00:14:31,696
That way you're buying time on purpose,

463
00:14:31,696 --> 00:14:33,736
instead of leaking money out of habit.

464
00:14:34,086 --> 00:14:35,305
Leak number twelve.

465
00:14:35,485 --> 00:14:37,110
Escalating vacations.

466
00:14:37,460 --> 00:14:38,381
With a raise,

467
00:14:38,381 --> 00:14:40,153
vacations often level up.

468
00:14:40,333 --> 00:14:41,428
A longer trip,

469
00:14:41,428 --> 00:14:42,523
a nicer hotel,

470
00:14:42,523 --> 00:14:43,852
a flight upgrade,

471
00:14:43,852 --> 00:14:45,964
a more distant destination.

472
00:14:46,144 --> 00:14:47,785
Travel can be one of the most

473
00:14:47,785 --> 00:14:49,766
rewarding things to spend money on.

474
00:14:49,946 --> 00:14:51,914
But many people put trips on credit

475
00:14:51,914 --> 00:14:54,050
cards and spend the next months paying

476
00:14:54,050 --> 00:14:54,555
them off.

477
00:14:54,906 --> 00:14:56,949
There's also a comparison trap.

478
00:14:57,129 --> 00:14:58,983
Each trip has to beat the last

479
00:14:58,983 --> 00:15:00,960
one, and the one you saw online.

480
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:03,830
That escalation can turn a joyful habit

481
00:15:03,830 --> 00:15:05,623
into a big yearly expense,

482
00:15:05,623 --> 00:15:06,934
financed with debt.

483
00:15:07,284 --> 00:15:09,458
Try this: set up a travel sinking

484
00:15:09,458 --> 00:15:11,566
fund and pay for trips with cash

485
00:15:11,566 --> 00:15:12,949
you've already saved.

486
00:15:13,129 --> 00:15:15,255
Decide the budget before you plan,

487
00:15:15,255 --> 00:15:15,881
not after.

488
00:15:16,061 --> 00:15:18,274
You'll enjoy the trip more when there's

489
00:15:18,274 --> 00:15:19,637
no bill waiting at home.

490
00:15:19,987 --> 00:15:21,496
Leak number thirteen.

491
00:15:21,676 --> 00:15:23,127
Kids' lifestyle creep.

492
00:15:23,477 --> 00:15:25,514
Parents want the best for their kids,

493
00:15:25,514 --> 00:15:27,056
and a raise can make it feel

494
00:15:27,056 --> 00:15:27,552
possible.

495
00:15:27,732 --> 00:15:30,266
More activities, private lessons,

496
00:15:30,266 --> 00:15:32,263
travel sports, newer gear,

497
00:15:32,263 --> 00:15:34,106
bigger birthday parties.

498
00:15:34,286 --> 00:15:35,876
Each choice comes from love.

499
00:15:36,056 --> 00:15:37,795
But the costs can rise fast,

500
00:15:37,795 --> 00:15:39,844
and kids quickly adapt to the new

501
00:15:39,844 --> 00:15:41,397
normal, just like adults.

502
00:15:41,747 --> 00:15:43,830
The hard truth is that kids often

503
00:15:43,830 --> 00:15:46,229
value time and attention more than the

504
00:15:46,229 --> 00:15:48,817
most expensive version of every activity.

505
00:15:48,997 --> 00:15:50,573
And one of the biggest gifts you

506
00:15:50,573 --> 00:15:52,297
can give them is parents who aren't

507
00:15:52,297 --> 00:15:53,332
stressed about money,

508
00:15:53,332 --> 00:15:55,154
and a retirement that won't depend on

509
00:15:55,154 --> 00:15:55,696
them later.

510
00:15:56,046 --> 00:15:58,340
Try this: let each child pick one

511
00:15:58,340 --> 00:16:00,287
or two activities they love,

512
00:16:00,287 --> 00:16:02,582
and set a yearly budget for them.

513
00:16:02,762 --> 00:16:05,258
Involve older kids in the decisions.

514
00:16:05,438 --> 00:16:07,411
It teaches them about trade offs,

515
00:16:07,411 --> 00:16:09,205
which might be one of the most

516
00:16:09,205 --> 00:16:10,639
valuable lessons of all.

517
00:16:10,990 --> 00:16:12,580
Leak number fourteen.

518
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:15,071
Gifts and social obligations.

519
00:16:15,421 --> 00:16:16,644
When you earn more,

520
00:16:16,644 --> 00:16:18,126
people may expect more.

521
00:16:18,306 --> 00:16:19,773
Bigger wedding gifts,

522
00:16:19,773 --> 00:16:22,079
more expensive birthday presents,

523
00:16:22,079 --> 00:16:24,315
picking up the check more often,

524
00:16:24,315 --> 00:16:25,922
helping family members.

525
00:16:26,102 --> 00:16:28,656
Some of this is generous and beautiful.

526
00:16:28,836 --> 00:16:30,782
Some of it happens because you feel

527
00:16:30,782 --> 00:16:32,783
you should, not because you want to.

528
00:16:33,134 --> 00:16:35,688
Try this: decide your giving on purpose.

529
00:16:35,868 --> 00:16:38,056
Set a yearly budget for gifts and

530
00:16:38,056 --> 00:16:39,978
help, and plan for it in your

531
00:16:39,978 --> 00:16:40,907
sinking funds.

532
00:16:41,087 --> 00:16:43,253
Generosity is one of the best uses

533
00:16:43,253 --> 00:16:43,827
of money.

534
00:16:44,007 --> 00:16:45,342
Just make it a choice,

535
00:16:45,342 --> 00:16:46,253
not a pressure,

536
00:16:46,253 --> 00:16:48,560
so it doesn't quietly replace your own

537
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:49,045
savings.

538
00:16:49,396 --> 00:16:52,065
And remember, the most meaningful gifts are

539
00:16:52,065 --> 00:16:53,680
rarely the most expensive.

540
00:16:53,860 --> 00:16:55,232
A handwritten note,

541
00:16:55,232 --> 00:16:57,761
time together, a shared experience.

542
00:16:57,941 --> 00:16:59,881
People remember how you made them feel

543
00:16:59,881 --> 00:17:01,412
much more than what you spent.

544
00:17:01,762 --> 00:17:03,271
Leak number fifteen.

545
00:17:03,451 --> 00:17:04,705
Status brands.

546
00:17:05,055 --> 00:17:06,223
With more income,

547
00:17:06,223 --> 00:17:08,422
it's tempting to signal success.

548
00:17:08,602 --> 00:17:11,182
Designer clothes, a luxury watch,

549
00:17:11,182 --> 00:17:12,746
a premium car badge.

550
00:17:12,926 --> 00:17:16,378
Economists have studied conspicuous consumption,

551
00:17:16,378 --> 00:17:18,247
spending meant to be seen,

552
00:17:18,247 --> 00:17:19,614
for over a century.

553
00:17:19,794 --> 00:17:22,428
Thorstein Veblen coined the term back in

554
00:17:22,428 --> 00:17:23,811
eighteen ninety nine.

555
00:17:24,161 --> 00:17:26,536
The trouble is that status spending is

556
00:17:26,536 --> 00:17:28,224
a race with no finish line.

557
00:17:28,404 --> 00:17:30,524
There's always a more expensive brand,

558
00:17:30,524 --> 00:17:32,644
and the people you're signaling to are

559
00:17:32,644 --> 00:17:34,708
usually too busy with their own lives

560
00:17:34,708 --> 00:17:35,266
to notice.

561
00:17:35,446 --> 00:17:37,230
Meanwhile, quiet wealth,

562
00:17:37,230 --> 00:17:39,608
the kind in investment accounts,

563
00:17:39,608 --> 00:17:42,284
is invisible but much more powerful.

564
00:17:42,634 --> 00:17:45,481
Try this: before buying a status item,

565
00:17:45,481 --> 00:17:46,755
ask who it's for.

566
00:17:46,935 --> 00:17:48,547
If it's genuinely for you,

567
00:17:48,547 --> 00:17:50,842
because you love the quality and will

568
00:17:50,842 --> 00:17:51,896
use it for years,

569
00:17:51,896 --> 00:17:52,206
fine.

570
00:17:52,386 --> 00:17:53,764
If it's for an audience,

571
00:17:53,764 --> 00:17:56,233
consider whether that audience is worth the

572
00:17:56,233 --> 00:17:56,577
money.

573
00:17:56,928 --> 00:17:58,049
Before the playbook,

574
00:17:58,049 --> 00:18:00,573
five signs you're on the lifestyle treadmill.

575
00:18:00,753 --> 00:18:00,985
One.

576
00:18:01,165 --> 00:18:03,119
You earn more than you did three

577
00:18:03,119 --> 00:18:05,439
years ago, but your savings balance is

578
00:18:05,439 --> 00:18:06,355
about the same.

579
00:18:06,535 --> 00:18:06,895
Two.

580
00:18:07,075 --> 00:18:08,849
You can't name where your last raise

581
00:18:08,849 --> 00:18:09,095
went.

582
00:18:09,275 --> 00:18:09,832
Three.

583
00:18:10,012 --> 00:18:12,487
Your fixed monthly costs have grown faster

584
00:18:12,487 --> 00:18:13,902
than your take home pay.

585
00:18:14,252 --> 00:18:14,786
Four.

586
00:18:14,966 --> 00:18:15,904
You often think,

587
00:18:15,904 --> 00:18:17,546
I'd be fine if I just earned

588
00:18:17,546 --> 00:18:18,367
a little more.

589
00:18:18,547 --> 00:18:19,523
And five.

590
00:18:19,703 --> 00:18:21,798
Most of your upgrades in the last

591
00:18:21,798 --> 00:18:24,210
year were things you'd now struggle to

592
00:18:24,210 --> 00:18:25,417
undo, like a lease,

593
00:18:25,417 --> 00:18:26,750
a loan or a contract.

594
00:18:26,930 --> 00:18:29,152
If three or more of these feel

595
00:18:29,152 --> 00:18:31,818
familiar, you're not bad with money.

596
00:18:31,998 --> 00:18:32,706
You're normal.

597
00:18:32,886 --> 00:18:35,704
But normal is exactly what the playbook

598
00:18:35,704 --> 00:18:37,077
is designed to fix.

599
00:18:37,427 --> 00:18:38,402
Quick pause.

600
00:18:38,582 --> 00:18:40,556
If you're counting your own leaks right

601
00:18:40,556 --> 00:18:41,670
now, you're not alone.

602
00:18:41,850 --> 00:18:43,825
Subscribe to the channel if this is

603
00:18:43,825 --> 00:18:44,277
helping.

604
00:18:44,457 --> 00:18:46,329
It's free, and it helps me keep

605
00:18:46,329 --> 00:18:48,021
making these videos for you.

606
00:18:48,201 --> 00:18:49,800
Now, the last five leaks,

607
00:18:49,800 --> 00:18:52,102
the ones that hurt your future most.

608
00:18:52,452 --> 00:18:53,322
Angle three.

609
00:18:53,502 --> 00:18:55,373
The five leaks that hurt your long

610
00:18:55,373 --> 00:18:57,298
term wealth, and then the rule that

611
00:18:57,298 --> 00:18:58,123
stops them all.

612
00:18:58,473 --> 00:18:59,924
Leak number sixteen.

613
00:19:00,104 --> 00:19:02,751
Not raising your retirement contributions.

614
00:19:03,101 --> 00:19:04,111
Here's a quiet one.

615
00:19:04,291 --> 00:19:07,547
Many people set their retirement contribution once,

616
00:19:07,547 --> 00:19:08,824
as a percent of pay,

617
00:19:08,824 --> 00:19:10,421
and never touch it again.

618
00:19:10,601 --> 00:19:11,663
If it's a percent,

619
00:19:11,663 --> 00:19:13,492
it grows a little with a raise.

620
00:19:13,672 --> 00:19:15,471
But if it's a flat dollar amount,

621
00:19:15,471 --> 00:19:17,435
a raise does nothing for your future

622
00:19:17,435 --> 00:19:17,816
at all.

623
00:19:17,996 --> 00:19:19,575
All of the raise goes to today.

624
00:19:19,926 --> 00:19:23,316
Many workplace plans offer an automatic increase

625
00:19:23,316 --> 00:19:26,282
feature, sometimes called auto escalation,

626
00:19:26,282 --> 00:19:29,390
that raises your contribution by one percent

627
00:19:29,390 --> 00:19:30,096
each year.

628
00:19:30,276 --> 00:19:32,082
It's one of the easiest ways to

629
00:19:32,082 --> 00:19:34,237
grow your savings without feeling it,

630
00:19:34,237 --> 00:19:36,625
because the increase usually comes with a

631
00:19:36,625 --> 00:19:36,975
raise.

632
00:19:37,325 --> 00:19:40,412
Try this: log into your retirement plan

633
00:19:40,412 --> 00:19:42,549
today and check two things.

634
00:19:42,729 --> 00:19:44,633
Are you getting the full employer match?

635
00:19:44,813 --> 00:19:46,903
And is automatic increase turned on?

636
00:19:47,083 --> 00:19:48,557
If not, turn it on.

637
00:19:48,737 --> 00:19:50,563
It takes five minutes and could be

638
00:19:50,563 --> 00:19:52,656
worth tens of thousands of dollars over

639
00:19:52,656 --> 00:19:53,300
your career.

640
00:19:53,650 --> 00:19:55,205
Leak number seventeen.

641
00:19:55,385 --> 00:19:56,744
Higher credit limits.

642
00:19:57,094 --> 00:19:58,663
When your income rises,

643
00:19:58,663 --> 00:20:01,325
card companies often raise your limits,

644
00:20:01,325 --> 00:20:02,690
and offer new cards.

645
00:20:02,870 --> 00:20:05,621
More available credit can make bigger purchases

646
00:20:05,621 --> 00:20:07,904
feel affordable, even if you don't have

647
00:20:07,904 --> 00:20:08,431
the cash.

648
00:20:08,611 --> 00:20:10,250
For people who pay in full,

649
00:20:10,250 --> 00:20:11,769
this doesn't matter much.

650
00:20:11,949 --> 00:20:13,881
For people who carry a balance,

651
00:20:13,881 --> 00:20:15,814
it can mean the debt grows with

652
00:20:15,814 --> 00:20:16,500
the income.

653
00:20:16,850 --> 00:20:18,579
Try this: treat your income,

654
00:20:18,579 --> 00:20:19,938
not your credit limit,

655
00:20:19,938 --> 00:20:21,482
as your spending ceiling.

656
00:20:21,662 --> 00:20:23,188
If you ever carry a balance,

657
00:20:23,188 --> 00:20:24,987
put the first part of every raise

658
00:20:24,987 --> 00:20:26,132
toward paying it off.

659
00:20:26,312 --> 00:20:29,038
Clearing high interest debt is like giving

660
00:20:29,038 --> 00:20:30,596
yourself a second raise.

661
00:20:30,946 --> 00:20:33,011
It's also worth checking how many cards

662
00:20:33,011 --> 00:20:33,964
you actually need.

663
00:20:34,144 --> 00:20:36,404
Every new card offer after a raise

664
00:20:36,404 --> 00:20:38,730
is designed to capture some of your

665
00:20:38,730 --> 00:20:39,462
new income.

666
00:20:39,642 --> 00:20:40,945
One or two cards,

667
00:20:40,945 --> 00:20:42,861
paid in full every month,

668
00:20:42,861 --> 00:20:46,004
cover almost everything most people need.

669
00:20:46,184 --> 00:20:49,169
More cards mostly mean more temptation and

670
00:20:49,169 --> 00:20:50,236
more due dates.

671
00:20:50,586 --> 00:20:51,990
Leak number eighteen.

672
00:20:52,170 --> 00:20:55,003
Financing upgrades against future raises.

673
00:20:55,354 --> 00:20:57,306
Some people spend a raise before they

674
00:20:57,306 --> 00:20:57,676
get it.

675
00:20:57,856 --> 00:20:59,304
They expect a promotion,

676
00:20:59,304 --> 00:21:01,175
so they buy the bigger house or

677
00:21:01,175 --> 00:21:02,261
the nicer car now,

678
00:21:02,261 --> 00:21:04,253
assuming the money will be there.

679
00:21:04,433 --> 00:21:06,390
But raises can be delayed,

680
00:21:06,390 --> 00:21:07,595
jobs can change,

681
00:21:07,595 --> 00:21:09,704
and companies can cut costs.

682
00:21:09,884 --> 00:21:12,795
Borrowing against future income turns a hope

683
00:21:12,795 --> 00:21:14,052
into an obligation.

684
00:21:14,402 --> 00:21:16,808
Try this: only upgrade after the raise

685
00:21:16,808 --> 00:21:18,138
has actually arrived,

686
00:21:18,138 --> 00:21:20,418
and after it's been in your paycheck

687
00:21:20,418 --> 00:21:22,064
for at least a few months.

688
00:21:22,244 --> 00:21:23,977
If you can live without the upgrade

689
00:21:23,977 --> 00:21:24,818
during that time,

690
00:21:24,818 --> 00:21:26,402
you might find you don't need it

691
00:21:26,402 --> 00:21:26,749
at all.

692
00:21:27,099 --> 00:21:28,909
A useful habit is to live on

693
00:21:28,909 --> 00:21:31,237
last year's income for the first few

694
00:21:31,237 --> 00:21:32,660
months of a new raise.

695
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:33,966
Bank the difference.

696
00:21:34,146 --> 00:21:35,778
If the raise turns out to be

697
00:21:35,778 --> 00:21:37,992
stable and you still want the upgrade,

698
00:21:37,992 --> 00:21:39,682
you'll be able to buy it with

699
00:21:39,682 --> 00:21:41,197
savings instead of a loan,

700
00:21:41,197 --> 00:21:43,179
which is a much stronger position.

701
00:21:43,529 --> 00:21:44,852
Leak number nineteen.

702
00:21:45,032 --> 00:21:47,517
Bonuses and windfalls with no plan.

703
00:21:47,867 --> 00:21:49,410
Bonuses, tax refunds,

704
00:21:49,410 --> 00:21:52,642
commissions and gifts often arrive without a

705
00:21:52,642 --> 00:21:53,010
plan.

706
00:21:53,190 --> 00:21:55,183
And money without a plan tends to

707
00:21:55,183 --> 00:21:57,660
disappear into the general spending pool.

708
00:21:57,840 --> 00:21:59,379
Many people can't say,

709
00:21:59,379 --> 00:22:00,359
a month later,

710
00:22:00,359 --> 00:22:02,739
what happened to their last bonus.

711
00:22:03,089 --> 00:22:05,631
Try this: set a simple windfall rule

712
00:22:05,631 --> 00:22:07,396
before the money arrives.

713
00:22:07,576 --> 00:22:10,184
For example, half to long term goals

714
00:22:10,184 --> 00:22:11,851
like investing or debt,

715
00:22:11,851 --> 00:22:13,952
a quarter to a near term goal

716
00:22:13,952 --> 00:22:15,836
like a trip or a car fund,

717
00:22:15,836 --> 00:22:17,793
and a quarter for pure fun.

718
00:22:17,973 --> 00:22:19,182
You get to enjoy it,

719
00:22:19,182 --> 00:22:21,177
and you still make real progress.

720
00:22:21,527 --> 00:22:23,259
And if the bonus is large,

721
00:22:23,259 --> 00:22:25,590
give yourself a cooling off period.

722
00:22:25,770 --> 00:22:27,804
Park it in a high yield savings

723
00:22:27,804 --> 00:22:31,018
account for thirty days before deciding anything.

724
00:22:31,198 --> 00:22:33,916
Big money decisions made in the excitement

725
00:22:33,916 --> 00:22:36,052
of a windfall are rarely the best

726
00:22:36,052 --> 00:22:36,376
ones.

727
00:22:36,556 --> 00:22:39,047
A month of patience costs nothing and

728
00:22:39,047 --> 00:22:40,259
often saves a lot.

729
00:22:40,610 --> 00:22:41,840
Leak number twenty.

730
00:22:42,020 --> 00:22:44,354
Locking in costs with contracts.

731
00:22:44,704 --> 00:22:47,071
The most dangerous upgrades are the ones

732
00:22:47,071 --> 00:22:48,372
you can't easily undo.

733
00:22:48,552 --> 00:22:50,430
Long leases, car loans,

734
00:22:50,430 --> 00:22:52,879
mortgages, annual memberships,

735
00:22:52,879 --> 00:22:56,470
phone contracts, private school commitments.

736
00:22:56,650 --> 00:22:58,351
Once signed, they stay,

737
00:22:58,351 --> 00:23:00,273
even if your income drops.

738
00:23:00,453 --> 00:23:04,040
Lifestyle inflation becomes lifestyle lock in.

739
00:23:04,390 --> 00:23:07,102
This is why many financial planners suggest

740
00:23:07,102 --> 00:23:09,626
keeping your fixed costs well below your

741
00:23:09,626 --> 00:23:11,455
income, so that if you lose a

742
00:23:11,455 --> 00:23:12,843
job or take a pay cut,

743
00:23:12,843 --> 00:23:14,421
you can still cover them.

744
00:23:14,601 --> 00:23:16,923
Flexibility is a form of wealth.

745
00:23:17,273 --> 00:23:20,240
Try this: before signing any contract after

746
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:21,137
a raise, ask,

747
00:23:21,137 --> 00:23:23,069
could I still pay this if my

748
00:23:23,069 --> 00:23:25,209
income went back to what it was

749
00:23:25,209 --> 00:23:25,899
last year?

750
00:23:26,079 --> 00:23:27,224
If the answer is no,

751
00:23:27,224 --> 00:23:29,399
you're betting the raise is permanent.

752
00:23:29,579 --> 00:23:31,321
Sometimes that's a good bet.

753
00:23:31,501 --> 00:23:32,917
Just make it on purpose.

754
00:23:33,267 --> 00:23:34,997
So how do you enjoy a raise

755
00:23:34,997 --> 00:23:36,471
and still build wealth?

756
00:23:36,651 --> 00:23:38,462
Here's the simplest rule I know.

757
00:23:38,812 --> 00:23:40,437
The fifty fifty raise rule.

758
00:23:40,617 --> 00:23:42,500
Every time your income goes up,

759
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:44,566
send at least half of the increase

760
00:23:44,566 --> 00:23:45,477
to your future,

761
00:23:45,477 --> 00:23:47,664
and enjoy the other half guilt free.

762
00:23:48,015 --> 00:23:49,350
Here's how it works.

763
00:23:49,530 --> 00:23:51,215
Your take home pay goes up three

764
00:23:51,215 --> 00:23:52,479
hundred dollars a month.

765
00:23:52,659 --> 00:23:55,442
You immediately send one hundred fifty to

766
00:23:55,442 --> 00:23:57,749
investing, debt payoff or savings,

767
00:23:57,749 --> 00:23:59,039
ideally on autopay,

768
00:23:59,039 --> 00:24:00,600
before you ever see it.

769
00:24:00,780 --> 00:24:03,046
The other one hundred fifty is yours

770
00:24:03,046 --> 00:24:03,613
to enjoy.

771
00:24:03,793 --> 00:24:04,999
A nicer dinner,

772
00:24:04,999 --> 00:24:07,009
a hobby, a small upgrade.

773
00:24:07,189 --> 00:24:08,059
No guilt.

774
00:24:08,410 --> 00:24:09,280
Why it works:

775
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:10,955
you still feel the raise,

776
00:24:10,955 --> 00:24:12,763
so you don't feel deprived.

777
00:24:12,943 --> 00:24:14,975
But your savings rate goes up every

778
00:24:14,975 --> 00:24:15,672
single year.

779
00:24:15,852 --> 00:24:17,830
Over a career with many raises,

780
00:24:17,830 --> 00:24:20,765
that steady increase can build serious wealth,

781
00:24:20,765 --> 00:24:23,189
without ever feeling like a sacrifice.

782
00:24:23,540 --> 00:24:26,365
Investing an extra one hundred fifty dollars

783
00:24:26,365 --> 00:24:27,842
a month from one raise,

784
00:24:27,842 --> 00:24:30,475
at a seven percent average yearly return,

785
00:24:30,475 --> 00:24:31,887
for twenty five years.

786
00:24:32,067 --> 00:24:33,553
And that's just one raise.

787
00:24:33,904 --> 00:24:35,053
And here's the magic.

788
00:24:35,233 --> 00:24:37,720
You'll probably get several raises over the

789
00:24:37,720 --> 00:24:38,414
next decade.

790
00:24:38,594 --> 00:24:40,608
If you apply the rule each time,

791
00:24:40,608 --> 00:24:41,740
the amounts stack.

792
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:43,079
One hundred fifty,

793
00:24:43,079 --> 00:24:44,302
then three hundred,

794
00:24:44,302 --> 00:24:46,684
then four hundred fifty a month going

795
00:24:46,684 --> 00:24:47,649
to your future,

796
00:24:47,649 --> 00:24:50,674
while your lifestyle still improves every year.

797
00:24:50,854 --> 00:24:53,246
That's what getting richer actually looks like.

798
00:24:53,596 --> 00:24:57,082
Let's see lifestyle inflation at three income

799
00:24:57,082 --> 00:24:57,624
levels.

800
00:24:57,804 --> 00:25:00,014
Because it doesn't disappear when you earn

801
00:25:00,014 --> 00:25:00,277
more.

802
00:25:00,457 --> 00:25:02,048
It just gets more expensive.

803
00:25:02,398 --> 00:25:04,452
At fifty thousand dollars a year,

804
00:25:04,452 --> 00:25:06,008
the trap looks like this.

805
00:25:06,188 --> 00:25:08,204
A car payment that's a little too

806
00:25:08,204 --> 00:25:10,098
high, a few streaming services,

807
00:25:10,098 --> 00:25:11,381
takeout twice a week,

808
00:25:11,381 --> 00:25:13,580
and a credit card that's never quite

809
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:14,130
paid off.

810
00:25:14,310 --> 00:25:16,300
The person feels like they'd be fine

811
00:25:16,300 --> 00:25:18,013
if they only earned a bit more.

812
00:25:18,363 --> 00:25:20,094
At one hundred thousand,

813
00:25:20,094 --> 00:25:21,393
they do earn more.

814
00:25:21,573 --> 00:25:23,945
Now there's a bigger apartment or a

815
00:25:23,945 --> 00:25:26,452
starter home, two car payments in the

816
00:25:26,452 --> 00:25:28,350
household, a gym membership,

817
00:25:28,350 --> 00:25:30,721
more travel, and nicer restaurants.

818
00:25:30,901 --> 00:25:32,616
Savings are still small,

819
00:25:32,616 --> 00:25:35,903
because the new lifestyle costs almost exactly

820
00:25:35,903 --> 00:25:37,833
what the new income brings.

821
00:25:38,013 --> 00:25:39,604
They feel like they'd be fine if

822
00:25:39,604 --> 00:25:40,996
they only earned a bit more.

823
00:25:41,347 --> 00:25:43,024
At two hundred thousand,

824
00:25:43,024 --> 00:25:44,423
the house is larger,

825
00:25:44,423 --> 00:25:46,310
the cars are luxury models,

826
00:25:46,310 --> 00:25:48,897
the kids are in expensive activities,

827
00:25:48,897 --> 00:25:51,134
and vacations are international.

828
00:25:51,314 --> 00:25:52,618
The numbers are bigger,

829
00:25:52,618 --> 00:25:54,205
but the feeling is the same.

830
00:25:54,385 --> 00:25:54,779
Tight.

831
00:25:54,959 --> 00:25:57,061
And notice the sentence they repeat.

832
00:25:57,241 --> 00:25:58,975
I'd be fine if I only earned

833
00:25:58,975 --> 00:25:59,656
a bit more.

834
00:25:59,836 --> 00:26:02,042
That sentence is the treadmill talking.

835
00:26:02,392 --> 00:26:05,200
Now picture the same three people following

836
00:26:05,200 --> 00:26:06,571
the fifty fifty rule.

837
00:26:06,751 --> 00:26:07,790
At fifty thousand,

838
00:26:07,790 --> 00:26:08,887
they save a little.

839
00:26:09,067 --> 00:26:10,621
At one hundred thousand,

840
00:26:10,621 --> 00:26:12,562
they save a meaningful amount.

841
00:26:12,742 --> 00:26:14,180
At two hundred thousand,

842
00:26:14,180 --> 00:26:15,978
they can save a large share of

843
00:26:15,978 --> 00:26:18,675
their income and still live very comfortably.

844
00:26:18,855 --> 00:26:20,668
The income is the same in both

845
00:26:20,668 --> 00:26:21,212
versions.

846
00:26:21,392 --> 00:26:23,499
The difference is whether the raises went

847
00:26:23,499 --> 00:26:25,246
to the treadmill or to the future.

848
00:26:25,596 --> 00:26:28,428
If you're already deep in lifestyle creep,

849
00:26:28,428 --> 00:26:30,182
here's a thirty day reset.

850
00:26:30,362 --> 00:26:32,312
Four weeks, four steps.

851
00:26:32,662 --> 00:26:33,335
Week one.

852
00:26:33,515 --> 00:26:33,701
Look.

853
00:26:33,881 --> 00:26:36,984
Download two months of statements and sort

854
00:26:36,984 --> 00:26:39,349
every expense into three groups.

855
00:26:39,529 --> 00:26:41,092
Needs, wants you love,

856
00:26:41,092 --> 00:26:43,082
and wants you barely notice.

857
00:26:43,262 --> 00:26:44,817
That third group is your target.

858
00:26:44,997 --> 00:26:47,059
Most people find a few hundred dollars

859
00:26:47,059 --> 00:26:49,392
a month there without cutting anything they

860
00:26:49,392 --> 00:26:50,315
truly care about.

861
00:26:50,665 --> 00:26:51,501
Week two.

862
00:26:51,681 --> 00:26:52,841
Cut the invisible.

863
00:26:53,021 --> 00:26:55,529
Cancel the subscriptions you don't use,

864
00:26:55,529 --> 00:26:56,557
call your phone,

865
00:26:56,557 --> 00:26:59,514
internet and insurance providers to lower your

866
00:26:59,514 --> 00:27:02,278
rates, and remove saved cards from shopping

867
00:27:02,278 --> 00:27:02,600
apps.

868
00:27:02,780 --> 00:27:05,058
These are one time actions that save

869
00:27:05,058 --> 00:27:06,576
money every month after.

870
00:27:06,927 --> 00:27:07,762
Week three.

871
00:27:07,942 --> 00:27:09,208
Automate the savings.

872
00:27:09,388 --> 00:27:11,238
Take the amount you freed up and

873
00:27:11,238 --> 00:27:13,724
set it to transfer automatically to savings

874
00:27:13,724 --> 00:27:15,054
or investing on payday.

875
00:27:15,234 --> 00:27:17,292
If the money disappears before you see

876
00:27:17,292 --> 00:27:18,484
it, you won't miss it.

877
00:27:18,664 --> 00:27:20,236
If it stays in checking,

878
00:27:20,236 --> 00:27:22,136
lifestyle creep will find it.

879
00:27:22,486 --> 00:27:23,461
And week four.

880
00:27:23,641 --> 00:27:24,964
Decide the next raise.

881
00:27:25,144 --> 00:27:26,267
Write down, today,

882
00:27:26,267 --> 00:27:28,511
what you'll do with your next raise.

883
00:27:28,691 --> 00:27:31,605
Fifty fifty, or whatever split you choose.

884
00:27:31,785 --> 00:27:34,518
Turn on automatic increases in your retirement

885
00:27:34,518 --> 00:27:34,816
plan.

886
00:27:34,996 --> 00:27:36,821
That way, when the raise comes,

887
00:27:36,821 --> 00:27:38,529
the decision is already made,

888
00:27:38,529 --> 00:27:40,708
and the treadmill doesn't get a vote.

889
00:27:41,058 --> 00:27:43,227
Let's look at two coworkers who got

890
00:27:43,227 --> 00:27:45,148
the same raises over ten years,

891
00:27:45,148 --> 00:27:47,814
from fifty thousand to eighty five thousand

892
00:27:47,814 --> 00:27:48,743
dollars a year.

893
00:27:48,923 --> 00:27:51,292
Ashley upgraded with every raise.

894
00:27:51,472 --> 00:27:53,035
A bigger apartment,

895
00:27:53,035 --> 00:27:53,858
a new car,

896
00:27:53,858 --> 00:27:55,256
more dinners out,

897
00:27:55,256 --> 00:27:56,244
nicer trips.

898
00:27:56,424 --> 00:27:58,141
At eighty five thousand,

899
00:27:58,141 --> 00:28:00,502
she had the same savings rate she

900
00:28:00,502 --> 00:28:01,432
had at fifty,

901
00:28:01,432 --> 00:28:02,433
close to zero,

902
00:28:02,433 --> 00:28:04,794
and still felt tight every month.

903
00:28:05,145 --> 00:28:07,815
Ben followed the fifty fifty rule.

904
00:28:07,995 --> 00:28:09,166
With each raise,

905
00:28:09,166 --> 00:28:11,949
half went to his 401k and investments,

906
00:28:11,949 --> 00:28:13,486
and half to his life.

907
00:28:13,666 --> 00:28:15,809
His apartment got a bit nicer,

908
00:28:15,809 --> 00:28:17,595
he upgraded his car once,

909
00:28:17,595 --> 00:28:18,810
he traveled more.

910
00:28:18,990 --> 00:28:21,186
But his savings rate climbed from five

911
00:28:21,186 --> 00:28:23,441
percent to almost twenty percent of his

912
00:28:23,441 --> 00:28:24,597
pay over the decade.

913
00:28:24,948 --> 00:28:26,036
After ten years,

914
00:28:26,036 --> 00:28:28,756
Ashley had a few thousand dollars saved.

915
00:28:28,936 --> 00:28:31,213
Ben had well over one hundred thousand

916
00:28:31,213 --> 00:28:33,731
dollars invested, depending on the market.

917
00:28:33,911 --> 00:28:35,699
Both enjoyed their lives.

918
00:28:35,879 --> 00:28:37,666
Both felt their income go up.

919
00:28:37,846 --> 00:28:39,726
But only one of them turned those

920
00:28:39,726 --> 00:28:40,865
raises into freedom.

921
00:28:41,215 --> 00:28:43,014
And here's the part people miss.

922
00:28:43,194 --> 00:28:44,831
Ben didn't feel deprived.

923
00:28:45,011 --> 00:28:46,567
He felt every raise.

924
00:28:46,747 --> 00:28:48,546
He just felt half of each one.

925
00:28:48,726 --> 00:28:50,650
Ashley felt every raise fully,

926
00:28:50,650 --> 00:28:51,740
for a few months,

927
00:28:51,740 --> 00:28:54,048
and then it disappeared into her new

928
00:28:54,048 --> 00:28:54,497
normal.

929
00:28:54,677 --> 00:28:55,687
The treadmill took it.

930
00:28:56,037 --> 00:28:57,383
One more thing about Ben.

931
00:28:57,563 --> 00:29:00,592
Because his fixed costs stayed reasonable,

932
00:29:00,592 --> 00:29:01,673
he had options.

933
00:29:01,853 --> 00:29:03,914
When his company went through layoffs in

934
00:29:03,914 --> 00:29:05,510
year eight, he wasn't panicked.

935
00:29:05,690 --> 00:29:06,799
He had savings,

936
00:29:06,799 --> 00:29:09,533
a manageable rent and no car payment.

937
00:29:09,713 --> 00:29:10,894
He took his time,

938
00:29:10,894 --> 00:29:12,213
found a better job,

939
00:29:12,213 --> 00:29:13,254
and kept going.

940
00:29:13,434 --> 00:29:15,493
Ashley, with higher fixed costs,

941
00:29:15,493 --> 00:29:17,102
felt she had to stay in a

942
00:29:17,102 --> 00:29:18,389
job she didn't like,

943
00:29:18,389 --> 00:29:20,962
because she couldn't afford even a short

944
00:29:20,962 --> 00:29:21,863
gap in income.

945
00:29:22,043 --> 00:29:25,004
Lifestyle inflation doesn't just cost money.

946
00:29:25,184 --> 00:29:26,612
It costs choices.

947
00:29:26,962 --> 00:29:29,148
A few questions people ask me about

948
00:29:29,148 --> 00:29:30,398
lifestyle inflation.

949
00:29:30,748 --> 00:29:31,583
Question one.

950
00:29:31,763 --> 00:29:34,213
Is all lifestyle inflation bad?

951
00:29:34,393 --> 00:29:34,556
No.

952
00:29:34,736 --> 00:29:37,601
Some upgrades genuinely improve your life,

953
00:29:37,601 --> 00:29:39,375
like a safer neighborhood,

954
00:29:39,375 --> 00:29:40,604
a shorter commute,

955
00:29:40,604 --> 00:29:41,900
better health care,

956
00:29:41,900 --> 00:29:43,606
or more time with family.

957
00:29:43,786 --> 00:29:45,922
The goal isn't to never upgrade.

958
00:29:46,102 --> 00:29:47,644
It's to upgrade on purpose,

959
00:29:47,644 --> 00:29:49,701
and make sure your savings grow too.

960
00:29:50,051 --> 00:29:50,933
Question two.

961
00:29:51,113 --> 00:29:53,632
What if I already inflated my lifestyle?

962
00:29:53,812 --> 00:29:55,869
Start with the leak that costs the

963
00:29:55,869 --> 00:29:57,562
most and adds the least joy.

964
00:29:57,742 --> 00:29:59,646
Often it's subscriptions,

965
00:29:59,646 --> 00:30:01,701
delivery, or a car payment.

966
00:30:01,881 --> 00:30:03,918
Undo one, and send that money to

967
00:30:03,918 --> 00:30:05,318
savings automatically.

968
00:30:05,498 --> 00:30:07,677
Then apply the fifty fifty rule to

969
00:30:07,677 --> 00:30:08,702
your next raise.

970
00:30:09,053 --> 00:30:10,202
Question three.

971
00:30:10,382 --> 00:30:12,367
What percent of income should I save?

972
00:30:12,547 --> 00:30:15,324
A common target for retirement is around

973
00:30:15,324 --> 00:30:17,684
fifteen percent of pre tax income,

974
00:30:17,684 --> 00:30:19,697
including any employer match,

975
00:30:19,697 --> 00:30:21,988
though it depends on your age and

976
00:30:21,988 --> 00:30:22,404
goals.

977
00:30:22,584 --> 00:30:24,030
If you're far from that,

978
00:30:24,030 --> 00:30:25,718
the raise rule is one of the

979
00:30:25,718 --> 00:30:27,646
most painless ways to get there.

980
00:30:27,996 --> 00:30:28,901
Question four.

981
00:30:29,081 --> 00:30:31,229
My partner spends every raise.

982
00:30:31,409 --> 00:30:32,396
What do I do?

983
00:30:32,576 --> 00:30:34,772
Talk about goals before you talk about

984
00:30:34,772 --> 00:30:35,293
spending.

985
00:30:35,473 --> 00:30:37,364
What do you both want in five

986
00:30:37,364 --> 00:30:38,212
or ten years?

987
00:30:38,392 --> 00:30:40,146
Then agree on a simple rule,

988
00:30:40,146 --> 00:30:41,210
like fifty fifty,

989
00:30:41,210 --> 00:30:42,212
and automate it.

990
00:30:42,392 --> 00:30:44,338
It's easier to agree on a system

991
00:30:44,338 --> 00:30:46,467
than to argue about every purchase.

992
00:30:46,818 --> 00:30:47,897
Question six.

993
00:30:48,077 --> 00:30:49,842
What if my raise is small,

994
00:30:49,842 --> 00:30:51,607
like two or three percent?

995
00:30:51,787 --> 00:30:53,852
Then the rule matters even more,

996
00:30:53,852 --> 00:30:56,369
because small raises are the easiest to

997
00:30:56,369 --> 00:30:57,789
lose without noticing.

998
00:30:57,969 --> 00:30:59,862
Even half of a small raise,

999
00:30:59,862 --> 00:31:01,755
sent to savings every year,

1000
00:31:01,755 --> 00:31:03,298
adds up over a career.

1001
00:31:03,478 --> 00:31:06,529
And small raises often roughly match inflation,

1002
00:31:06,529 --> 00:31:08,996
so not spending them on upgrades keeps

1003
00:31:08,996 --> 00:31:11,268
your budget from slipping backward.

1004
00:31:11,619 --> 00:31:12,791
And question five.

1005
00:31:12,971 --> 00:31:15,759
Does lifestyle inflation happen to rich people

1006
00:31:15,759 --> 00:31:16,001
too?

1007
00:31:16,181 --> 00:31:16,948
Absolutely.

1008
00:31:17,128 --> 00:31:18,259
Many high earners,

1009
00:31:18,259 --> 00:31:20,585
like doctors, lawyers and executives,

1010
00:31:20,585 --> 00:31:23,225
struggle with it because their peer groups

1011
00:31:23,225 --> 00:31:24,105
spend heavily.

1012
00:31:24,285 --> 00:31:27,327
Income doesn't protect you from lifestyle inflation.

1013
00:31:27,507 --> 00:31:28,540
A plan does.

1014
00:31:28,891 --> 00:31:30,181
Before we wrap up,

1015
00:31:30,181 --> 00:31:31,329
one honest note.

1016
00:31:31,509 --> 00:31:33,611
Enjoying your money is part of the

1017
00:31:33,611 --> 00:31:33,982
point.

1018
00:31:34,162 --> 00:31:35,590
You worked for that raise.

1019
00:31:35,770 --> 00:31:37,550
The goal of this video isn't to

1020
00:31:37,550 --> 00:31:39,445
make you feel guilty about a nice

1021
00:31:39,445 --> 00:31:41,110
dinner or a better apartment.

1022
00:31:41,290 --> 00:31:43,123
It's to make sure that years from

1023
00:31:43,123 --> 00:31:45,233
now, your raises added up to something

1024
00:31:45,233 --> 00:31:45,899
you can see,

1025
00:31:45,899 --> 00:31:47,954
not just a lifestyle you can't afford

1026
00:31:47,954 --> 00:31:48,454
to leave.

1027
00:31:48,804 --> 00:31:49,628
Quick recap.

1028
00:31:49,808 --> 00:31:51,141
The money leaks:

1029
00:31:51,141 --> 00:31:52,891
taxes, housing, cars,

1030
00:31:52,891 --> 00:31:55,058
subscriptions, eating out,

1031
00:31:55,058 --> 00:31:58,225
premium everything, and tech upgrades.

1032
00:31:58,405 --> 00:31:59,506
The mind leaks:

1033
00:31:59,506 --> 00:32:01,121
the hedonic treadmill,

1034
00:32:01,121 --> 00:32:02,368
a new peer group,

1035
00:32:02,368 --> 00:32:05,011
weekly treats, convenience spending,

1036
00:32:05,011 --> 00:32:08,240
escalating vacations, kids' lifestyle creep,

1037
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:09,855
gifts and obligations,

1038
00:32:09,855 --> 00:32:11,176
and status brands.

1039
00:32:11,526 --> 00:32:12,979
And the future leaks:

1040
00:32:12,979 --> 00:32:15,125
not raising your contributions,

1041
00:32:15,125 --> 00:32:16,579
higher credit limits,

1042
00:32:16,579 --> 00:32:19,347
upgrades financed against future raises,

1043
00:32:19,347 --> 00:32:20,801
bonuses with no plan,

1044
00:32:20,801 --> 00:32:22,462
and locked in contracts.

1045
00:32:22,642 --> 00:32:24,151
One rule stops them all.

1046
00:32:24,331 --> 00:32:26,201
Half of every raise to your future.

1047
00:32:26,381 --> 00:32:27,356
Half to enjoy.

1048
00:32:27,706 --> 00:32:29,346
If you want the full plan for

1049
00:32:29,346 --> 00:32:31,439
turning every raise into real wealth,

1050
00:32:31,439 --> 00:32:32,570
it's in my playbook.

1051
00:32:32,750 --> 00:32:34,294
The link is in the description.

1052
00:32:34,474 --> 00:32:36,354
And watch my video on why most

1053
00:32:36,354 --> 00:32:37,481
people stay broke.

1054
00:32:37,661 --> 00:32:40,576
Lifestyle inflation is one of the biggest

1055
00:32:40,576 --> 00:32:41,144
reasons.

1056
00:32:41,324 --> 00:32:42,462
I'm Ryan Rover.

1057
00:32:42,642 --> 00:32:43,873
See you in the next one.
